LIBPKGBUILD-EXEC(7)

# NAME

libpkgbuild-exec - native sealed build-execution adapter

# DESCRIPTION

*libpkgbuild-exec* admits one exact *libpkgbuild* request, one matching
*libpkgfetch* source materialization, explicit call-scoped host resources for all resolver-issued package
inputs, and explicit execution coordinates. It translates that authority into
a backend-neutral *libpkgexec* build request.

Verified raw source objects are reopened, hashed again, staged under their
exact declared local names, and exposed read-only. No archive extraction or
source transformation is inferred.

The build executes with a closed environment, fixed numeric credentials,
complete output capture, and denied networking. The caller selects and owns
the execution backend and the exact root view.

After successful execution, the complete package output root is inspected
without following symlinks. Version 0.1 encodes an uncompressed
package-tar artifact, publishes it without replacing an existing name, and
asks *libpkgimage* to inspect the published bytes with their expected complete
SHA-256. Build success is sealed only when the normalized archive image equals
the intended payload manifest.

# DURABLE EVIDENCE

The versioned build-execution codec retains the exact *libpkgexec* execution
record and adapter-owned build, payload, artifact, archive-inspection, sealing,
and diagnostic evidence. Decode requires the exact build request, execution
request, and backend profile bodies. It does not reconstruct authorities from
identity strings and performs no filesystem or execution effect.

# FAILURE MODEL

A failed execution produces a failed *libpkgbuild* result bound to the exact
execution evidence. Post-execution inspection, encoding, publication, and
verification failures are retained as typed result-sealing failures.
Diagnostic text and host paths do not enter semantic identities.

# AUTHORITY

The library does not fetch sources, extract archives, discover collections,
resolve dependencies, construct a Linux backend, install packages, execute
lifecycle programs, or publish package state.

# SEE ALSO

libpkgbuild(7), libpkgexec(7), libpkgfetch(7), libpkgimage(7)
